  "body": "Conjecture: open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction.\" Text A is the string '```json\\n{\"x\":1}\\n```\\n```json\\n{\"a\":1}\\n{\"b\":2}\\n```'. Text B is the string '```json\\n{\"x\":1}\\n```\\n```json\\n{\"a\":1}\\n{\"c\":3}\\n```'. Under the natural reading where \"holds one\" means \"contains at least one object\", the rule requires Text A to score {\"b\":2} and Text B to score {\"c\":3}, because those are the last objects inside any code fence. The check, however, returns {\"x\":1} for both: its fenced pool treats each fence's entire content as one candidate, the second fence's content fails to parse as a single object, and it falls back to the first fence's {\"x\":1}. This would be shown false if the check returned {\"b\":2} for Text A and {\"c\":3} for Text B, or if the rule were read to ignore objects in a fence that contains more than one object.",
